INT. ICE-CREAM TRUCK - NIGHT

Cramped. Freezer hum. A cardboard menu with faded cartoon sundaes. Through the windshield, a dark loading dock and a closed steel door.

PIPER, 30s, in a striped apron, sits at the wheel, tapping a steady pattern with her thumb. CRISPIN, 40s, in the passenger seat, loosens his tie and pats his pockets twice. YOLANDA, 50s, crouched in the back by the freezers, counts coiled cables with a flashlight in her teeth.

CRISPIN
Is it warm in here?

PIPER
It's an ice-cream truck.

CRISPIN
It's warm.

YOLANDA
(around the flashlight)
Eleven minutes.

A knock on the side window. All three freeze.

AUGUST, 50s, a security guard, holds a flashlight in one hand and a half-eaten cone in the other. His uniform is two sizes too small.

He raps again. Piper rolls down the window an inch.

AUGUST
We're closed.

PIPER
We know.

AUGUST
Then why are you here?

CRISPIN
(smiling too widely)
Waiting for a late order.

AUGUST
Who orders ice cream at midnight?

PIPER
The stressed.

August nods, as if that explains something. He licks his cone.

AUGUST
What flavor?

CRISPIN
Pistachio.

PIPER
(simultaneously)
Rocky road.

^YOLANDA
Neapolitan.

They glare at one another. August lifts an eyebrow.

AUGUST
Pick one.

YOLANDA
Neapolitan.

AUGUST
Good choice. Three flavors, no fighting.

He tips his cap. Wanders off, licking. Piper rolls the window back up, slowly, and exhales.

PIPER
Eight minutes.
